{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-32167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times 2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\n- $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution\n- $Ax=0$ has infinitely many solutions\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "4713558b4f351438f7b24d355d9b9bb43b537f322660cc6b17328e79c53de0f3"} {"question" "q-32167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and III give us true statements:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution.* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $det(A)\\ne 0.$* $\\,✓$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "4713558b4f351438f7b24d355d9b9bb43b537f322660cc6b17328e79c53de0f3"} {"question" "q-32167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7d3087baf55591da19e797c183909e7f6b4eb60e0a79522f295cd288037478e"} {"question" "q-32167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "4713558b4f351438f7b24d355d9b9bb43b537f322660cc6b17328e79c53de0f3"} {"question" "q-32167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "4713558b4f351438f7b24d355d9b9bb43b537f322660cc6b17328e79c53de0f3"} {"question" "q-32167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "4713558b4f351438f7b24d355d9b9bb43b537f322660cc6b17328e79c53de0f3"} {"question" "q-47653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $Ax=0$ has no solutions\n- The lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2$ intersect at infinitely many points" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "4713558b4f351438f7b24d355d9b9bb43b537f322660cc6b17328e79c53de0f3"} {"question" "q-47653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\cdot k-2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6k+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $6k+6\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $Ax=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement I is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "4713558b4f351438f7b24d355d9b9bb43b537f322660cc6b17328e79c53de0f3"} {"question" "q-47653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7d3087baf55591da19e797c183909e7f6b4eb60e0a79522f295cd288037478e"} {"question" "q-47653" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "4713558b4f351438f7b24d355d9b9bb43b537f322660cc6b17328e79c53de0f3"} {"question" "q-47653" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "4713558b4f351438f7b24d355d9b9bb43b537f322660cc6b17328e79c53de0f3"} {"question" "q-47653" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "4713558b4f351438f7b24d355d9b9bb43b537f322660cc6b17328e79c53de0f3"} {"question" "q-73608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If $k\\ne 1$, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "4713558b4f351438f7b24d355d9b9bb43b537f322660cc6b17328e79c53de0f3"} {"question" "q-73608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nLet $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}].$ If $k\\ne 1$, then\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]=1-k\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSo, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is invertible and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the unique solution of  $Ax=0.$\nAlso, the system \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nhas exactly one solution, and the lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$ intersect at exactly one point. \n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If $k\\ne 1$, then the system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the unique zero solution.* $\\,✓$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "4713558b4f351438f7b24d355d9b9bb43b537f322660cc6b17328e79c53de0f3"} {"question" "q-73608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7d3087baf55591da19e797c183909e7f6b4eb60e0a79522f295cd288037478e"} {"question" "q-73608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "4713558b4f351438f7b24d355d9b9bb43b537f322660cc6b17328e79c53de0f3"} {"question" "q-73608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]=0"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has no solution"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ is a solution for the system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "The lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$ are parallel"] ["text" "The system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the unique zero solution"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "4713558b4f351438f7b24d355d9b9bb43b537f322660cc6b17328e79c53de0f3"} {"question" "q-73608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the unique zero solution"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "4713558b4f351438f7b24d355d9b9bb43b537f322660cc6b17328e79c53de0f3"} {"question" "q-128124" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "4713558b4f351438f7b24d355d9b9bb43b537f322660cc6b17328e79c53de0f3"} {"question" "q-128124" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statements II and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "4713558b4f351438f7b24d355d9b9bb43b537f322660cc6b17328e79c53de0f3"} {"question" "q-128124" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7d3087baf55591da19e797c183909e7f6b4eb60e0a79522f295cd288037478e"} {"question" "q-128124" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "4713558b4f351438f7b24d355d9b9bb43b537f322660cc6b17328e79c53de0f3"} {"question" "q-128124" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_comple